wayfire 0.7.4 (Debian 0.7.4-3+deb12u1) for LP: keys to the input method's
keyboard grab, as sway 1.7 does it.

wayfire 0.7.4 relays text-input-v3 to an input method (input-method-v2)
and lets the input method grab the keyboard, but never sends the grab a
key. LP's input method is lp-osk (desktop/osk/type.c), which types Korean
from the laptop's keys through that grab. Without keys, every application
that types through text-input-v3 - Electron and Chromium on Wayland, foot -
got English only under wayfire, whatever the language was set to; GTK and
Qt applications were not affected (they reach fcitx5 through their own
input method modules).

With the patch:

- A key no binding takes goes to the grab, not to the application, while
  an input method holds it (keyboard.cpp). The input method's own virtual
  keyboard is left out (input_method_relay::grab_for): that is how it
  sends back out the keys it does not want, and they are the
  application's.
- A key the application saw go down before the grab began is let go to
  the application.
- The seat's keyboard is not switched for a key that goes to the grab.
  Each switch sends every client the keymap again, and with the input
  method's virtual keyboard carrying keys back out it would be two
  switches for every key.
- Modifiers go to the grab while it is there; the grab gets the keyboard
  in use and its modifiers when it begins, and the application the
  modifiers back when it ends.

And the place plugin (plugins/single_plugins/place.cpp): a new window
bigger than the work area - Chrome opens 1018 pixels tall on a 1080
screen - is made to fit it, and no window is placed past the work area's
top or left edge. Centred as it was, Chrome's top went under the top bar,
and with it the tab strip's minimize, maximize and close buttons.

And xdg-decoration (src/core/core.cpp): a window gets the decoration mode
it asks for, and preferred_decoration_mode only when it asks for none.
0.7.4 read its own pending answer in place of the request, so every window
got preferred_decoration_mode - LP's is "client", and Chrome's pop-up
windows (window.open, the Google sign-in), which ask for a server frame
and draw none themselves, had no title bar and no close button.

And the decoration plugin (plugins/decor), the frame wayfire draws for
those windows and for X11 programs: the title at a header bar's size
(it was 80% of the bar's height) with a margin and an ellipsis, and the
minimise, maximise and close buttons as light marks on the dark bar
(they were grey discs with a black glyph at a quarter opacity), drawn
at twice the size so they stay sharp at scale 2.

